  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nCURRENTS-MONDAY: Another dreary and wet day continues! Taking a look \nacross the area, we're continuing to enjoy a brief break this \nafternoon as the rain shield lifts into northeastern SD and central \nSD. While these conditions will likely persist through late \nafternoon, should see a shift eastwards during the evening hours \nlocking us into intermittent showers for most of the night. From \nhere, should see rain coverage continue to expand into Monday \nmorning as a developing mid-level low lifts out of the Nebraska \npanhandle into central and eastern SoDAK. Given the better lift and \nstrengthening warm air advection (WAA) with the wave, pockets of \nmoderate (and at times heavy) rain likely develop throughout the day \nmainly west of I-29. Otherwise, this will be a longer duration \nrainfall event (24-30 hours) so flooding concerns are not expecting. \nHowever, localized ponding in urban and low-lying areas will be \npossible at times especially where the heaviest accumulations \ndevelops. \n\nLooking at overall amounts, most deterministic and ensemble guidance \ncontinues to show high agreement in areas along west of a Yankton to \nSioux Falls to Windom, MN line being the focus for 1+ inches amounts \nduring the period with some low to moderate potential (30%-50%) for \nlocally higher (up to 2 inches) west of the James River Valley. As \nthis activity continues to gradually lift northeastwards, could also \nsee some convectively enhanced showers develop by Monday evening \nmainly along and east of I-29 as the dry slot lifts through portions \nof the area and some weak instability develops. Conditions should \ngradually clear from southwest to northeast starting as early as \nMonday evening. Lastly, a large temperature gradient will set up \nacross the region on Monday due to the increased precipitation \nchances. As a result, daily highs will vary between the mid 40s to \nmid 50s in the wettest areas (west of the line) and mid 50s to low \n60s in the driest areas (lower Missouri River Valley and \nnorthwestern IA).\n\nTUESDAY & WEDNESDAY: Looking into Tuesday, any lingering showers \nshould continue to taper off northeastwards during the morning hours \nbringing the return of quieter conditions. Cloud cover should \ngradually decrease west of I-29 as southerly surface winds return. \nThis combined with a bit of lingering WAA will help temperatures \nslightly recover with highs expected to be in the low 60s to low 70s.\nA tightening SPG could also lead to some marginal breeziness in the \nafternoon with gusts between 20-30 mph expected. By Wednesday, could \nsee the return of some scattered showers during the afternoon hours \nas a quick shortwave drags a cold front through the Northern Plains. \nHowever, any resulting accumulations would be light with most \nensemble guidance showing low to moderate confidence (30%-50%) in \nmeasurable precipitation mainly east of I-29. Lastly, we'll see \nsimilar temperatures to Tuesday with highs peaking in the low 60s to \nlow 70s. \n\nTHURSDAY ONWARDS: Heading into the extended period, quasi-zonal flow \nwill return through about Friday before Upper-level ridging returns \nto the plains. Some medium to long-range deterministic guidance \ncontinues to latch on to some additional precipitation chances \nbetween Friday and Saturday. However, there are some timing \ndiscrepancies among guidance. We'll just continue to watch this \nperiod for now especially since its a big holiday for the kiddos. \nOtherwise, we'll continue to lean slightly above normal temperature-\nwise through Sunday with highs mainly in the 60s with some low 70s \nacross northwestern IA at times.",
